<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Merged into the bundle's Info.plist by the Tauri bundler on macOS. Every key
  here is a TCC usage string: the sentence macOS puts in front of the user when
  it asks. They are read by someone deciding whether to say yes, so each one
  says what the capability UNLOCKS and, where it matters, what Atlas will not do
  with it. Keep them true — a usage string that overstates is the first lie the
  product tells, and it is told at the least forgivable moment.

  A missing key is NOT a soft failure. For Bluetooth in particular, the first
  framework call without NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription raises SIGABRT — the
  app dies on launch rather than returning nil (verified on macOS 26.3; see
  docs/decisions/010-objc2-wifi-bluetooth-resolution.md). So the key has to land
  in the same change as the code that touches the framework, never after it.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Atlas uses your microphone for voice conversations and wake-word detection.</string>

  <!--
    Read-only, and the string says so because it is the whole truth: macOS
    exposes Bluetooth power as a getter with no setter anywhere in any public
    framework, so Atlas cannot turn it on or off, pair, or control a device even
    if it wanted to. The status bar shows state and connected devices, and hands
    the on/off switch to System Settings.
  -->
  <key>NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Atlas shows your Bluetooth status and which devices are connected. It never pairs with, connects to, or controls them.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
